[#:seon.task{:name "adoption-readopts-what-the-jvm-loaded"
             :plan "A move to HEAD spends 6.4-9.9 s adopting after readiness (development-source-refresh! 5,171 ms, adopt-entities! 2,761 ms) re-adopting what the fresh JVM already loaded; boot's store->branch layer is 27-32 s whenever the commit touches analyzer reach (full-source-refresh! :all: lint ~8 s, analysis ~8 s, three transactions ~10 s); today's nuke was ready in 43 s. Both are over the 10-second start law. 2026-10-07 start --head onto 108dd1c3e: ready 51.0 s, `:seon.source/current` 44.5 s publishing 132 of 847 changed inputs (.clj-kondo/config.edn among them, which reselects every source), then a post-readiness adoption of 20.8 s (refresh-source! -> development-source-refresh! 20.5 s, adopt-entities! 15.8 s, one 15.0 s transact! with write-report validation 4.5 s, published-changes 3.8 s). An unchanged restart selects 0 paths in 79 ms, so the publication is catch-up; the re-adoption is per move. Read `seon.cluster/adopt-entities!`, `seon.cluster/development-source-refresh!`, `seon.cluster/full-source-refresh!`, `docs/research/agent-platform/landing/inv-boot-operator-opus-2026-09-26.md`, `docs/research/agent-platform/perf-profile-2026-09-26.md` and `docs/seon/issues/move-to-head-takes-a-minute-or-more-not-seconds.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-missed-quiescence-keeps-the-route-listener"
             :plan "A stop that misses db<->flow quiescence keeps the route listener: a wedged proc cannot run its own stop transition. Recommended option 2 queues flow/stop even on a miss (about 15 lines in stop-flows!). Read `seon.cluster/stop-flows!`, `docs/research/agent-platform/landing/inv-lifecycle-storage-opus-2026-09-26.md` and `docs/seon/issues/a-cluster-stop-that-misses-armer-quiescence-keeps-the-route-listener.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "adoption-write-costs-sixteen-seconds"
             :plan "start --head spends 15.9 s in the adoption write, mostly seon.db with-declarations x18,794; over the one-second rule. Cost must follow the changed declarations. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/landing/fix-dial-retirement-adoption-2026-09-26.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "boot-publication-phases-are-invisible"
             :plan "A boot resume publication runs before arm-host-program!, so no profile cell observes it and refresh-source!'s explain-slow says nothing; *source-progress!* is bound nowhere (report-source-progress! is a no-op outside tests), so its phase lines vanish. The 132 s store->branch layer of the e405f51be boot could only be split by replaying the publication on a scratch branch (tmp/perf-boot-opus/replay.clj). Carry the boot publication's phase milliseconds in its result and the instance's readiness. Still true 2026-10-07: the 44.5 s `:seon.source/current` layer of the 108dd1c3e boot has no profile cell; seon.log has two lines for the whole boot. Read `seon.cluster/populate-source!`, `seon.cluster/refresh-source!` and `docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-boot-opus-2026-09-26.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-test-adoption-moves-the-jvms-loaded-identity"
             :plan "`seon.sci.eval/loaded-identity` is one process-wide atom, and call-with-program-change (from development-source-refresh!) advances it to the commit the adopting store published. A test that boots a cluster on its own root and store and adopts there (seon.adoption-contract-freshness-test calls refresh-source! with a development cluster) therefore renames the JVM's loaded program after a commit of another store, and later test requests' `seon.test/arm-first-loads!` open that commit through `seon.cluster.source/database`. That is JVM-global test state (CLAUDE.md). Unconfirmed instance: shrink-test saw requests at 18:20:59 and 18:24:08 CST open 6ab855c9 (default's 17:31 publication) through that path although the cluster record named 6ab86150; the note attributes them to pid 50733, which started only at 18:26:24 CST, so they ran on the JVM before it. Key the loaded identity by the store whose program the JVM loaded, and never let an adoption on another store move it. Read `seon.cluster/development-source-refresh!` and `docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-boot-opus-2026-09-26.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "incremental-publication-test-redefines-process-wide-vars"
             :plan "`seon.incremental-publication-test/non-program-inputs-have-their-own-publication-owner` points publication at a fixture checkout with with-redefs-fn over clojure.java.io/resource and `seon.cluster/publication-roots`. Both are Var roots, so every thread of the shared JVM (default's agents, other test members) reads the fixture's schemas and roots while it runs. Give refresh-source! a source seam (publication roots and the schema resource directory passed as values) so the test hands its checkout, or declare the test isolated until then. Read `seon.cluster/refresh-source!` and `test/seon/incremental_publication_test.clj`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "schema-adoption-refuses-instead-of-dropping-an-edn-encoded-value"
             :plan "invalid-value-retraction decodes each stored value with `seon.schema.datahike/decode-attribute-value-in`, which for an EDN-encoded attribute validates the decoded value against the attribute's NEW form and throws ::schema-invalid (datahike.clj:328-331). So a narrowed EDN-encoded attribute makes the whole adoption throw instead of retracting only the refused values, and an EDN-encoded cardinality-many attribute would validate one member against its whole [:vector ...] form. No installed many attribute is EDN-encoded today (117 probed on default, 2026-09-27), so the many case is latent. Decode without the slot validation here (the retraction validates), or share the writer's attribute plan (db.clj write-attribute-plan). Read `seon.cluster/invalid-value-retraction` and `docs/research/agent-platform/landing/register-anywhere-diagnosis-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "schema-convergence-sees-order-the-store-does-not-keep"
             :plan "schema-entity-changes reports `:seon.agent/agent`, :seon.issue/issue and `:seon.config.ai/thinking` as unconverged on a fresh member branch, so every open re-transacts them: `:seon.render/units` is stored as a cardinality-many set (read back sorted) while the desired entity holds the declared vector, and store-comparable-value compares order since dd5dcaff7 (vector storage S9). cluster-test schema-entity-convergence-uses-the-stores-own-semantics is red (probed on default 18:07 local 2026-09-27). Compare a set-stored attribute as a set; order only where storage keeps it (a vector-cell attribute). Read `seon.cluster/schema-entity-changes`, `seon.cluster/schema-entity-converged?` and `test/seon/cluster_test.clj`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "publication-tests-cost-35-seconds"
             :plan "cluster.source-test flat-scratch-write-refusal-retires-the-candidate (35.6 s), incremental-publication-does-not-change-an-existing-cluster (40.0 s) and publication-hands-its-commit-projection-unstamped (35.7 s) pass but exceed their 5 s bound (r8 at 7533e982d); the long work-on-an-acquired-branch test then had 1.5 s of its request left. reconcile-test apply-then-reapply-converges-over-generated-populations took 35.3 s. Each over ten seconds needs its responsible armed functions named and fixed or owner authorization. Read `seon.cluster.source/publish!`, `seon.reconcile/reconcile!`, `test/seon/cluster/source_test.clj` and `test/seon/reconcile_test.clj`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "schema-form-keys-re-derive-files-whose-entities-do-not-change"
             :plan "A start onto 0f92ebdbc (34 changed files: 25 schema resources from e941b7845, repl.clj, fn.clj, 3 tests) was ready in 20.97 s against 5.97 s on an unchanged program: source current 17.3 s. The publication changed 106 derivation keys; 97 of those files had unchanged bytes and their re-derived entities asserted NOTHING but the new `:seon.fn.file/derivation-digest` (194 of the transaction's 2,145 datoms, zero other attributes; current-src tx 536870951, measured 2026-09-28). Re-analysis costs about 80 ms per file (106 files: 8.3 s armed, `seon.fn.analyzer/analyze` 4.7 s of it, invoke-kondo 2.3 s), so about 7.6 s of that boot re-derived identical entities. The key (derivation-keys) digests the whole form of every schema key a file's keywords and contracts name, closed over schema dependencies, so an edit that only drops render pairs from 82 error schemas re-keys every file whose contracts name one. Also, a publication whose keys are stale analyzes the changed files twice: full-source-refresh! analyzes `changed`, finds `stale`, then recurses and analyzes changed plus stale again. Done when a schema edit re-derives only files whose derived entities read what changed (key on what the derivation actually reads from the forms, or cut off early when the re-derived rows equal the stored ones before the rest of the publication), the changed files are analyzed once, and the same probe shows the boot source layer proportional to files whose entities change. Read `seon.cluster/derivation-keys`, `seon.cluster/derivation-schema-keys`, `seon.cluster/full-source-refresh!` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-profile--evidence.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "retiring-an-attribute-with-stored-component-data-refuses-adoption"
             :plan "Removing `:seon.maintenance.result/collect-branches` (a component vector with no reader; its {`:seon.store/branch` `:seon.source/commit-id`} elements broke boot, fixed by 919be10c5) cannot adopt on default: the cluster population transaction refuses 'Complete component validation refused: vector-element-swept' (entity 106090, cell 106091) because the overnight collection receipt still holds the vector. Retiring a schema attribute must retract its stored values in the same population transaction (as drop-retired-families! does for retired identity families), not refuse. Done when that removal adopts on the live store and the receipt stops storing heads. Read `seon.cluster/populate-source!` and `seon.maintenance/project-collect-result`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-missed-agent-join-throws-instead-of-answering"
             :plan "stop-flows! declares [:or :nil `:seon.await/timeout-error`] and its docstring says a join that misses its bound answers the await owner's timeout value, but stop-graph! throws turn-completion-failure!'s ex-info (agent.clj:1402,1523-1530) and stop-flows! has no handling around `seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow!`. Only the view, db<->flow and cluster-graph joins answer a value; an agent join throws through `seon.cluster.boot/stop!` and `seon.cluster.host/stop!`. disarm! and stop-flows! must return the typed timeout error naming the missing exit, keep the resources, and every caller (stop-owned-graphs!, arm!, `seon.cluster.boot/stop!`, `seon.cluster.host/stop!`) handle the returned value. Proof: a stuck agent's disarm! and a cluster stop with one stuck agent return the typed error and still stop the other agents. Read `seon.cluster/stop-flows!` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/inventory/q14-exit-deadline.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "contract-instruction-entity-changes"
             :plan "Give `seon.cluster/instruction-entity-changes` a complete Malli contract that checks what its boundary is for, with the cheapest schema that checks it: registered names, every input and output, the error variants it returns. A contract that refuses a real value (a reaching test, a live call, your probe) is a finding, not friction: if the contract misread the value's shape, fix the contract and cite the observed shape; if the producer emits a bad value, keep the contract, send root the finding with `my.message/send`, naming `seon.cluster/instruction-entity-changes` with the exact form, the refused value's essence and the Malli explain, and say so in your reply. Never widen to :any, [:maybe ...] or :map to go green. Done when `seon.contracts/gaps` answers [] for it and its reaching tests stay green and no slower. Read `docs/seon/architecture/contracts-guide.md`."}
